--   migrations/0007_custom_categories.sql
--   migrations/0008_sms_ignore.sql
--   migrations/0009_harden_function_security.sql
--   migrations/0010_fix_function_grants.sql
--
-- 0003_convert_legacy_transfers.sql is deliberately LEFT OUT. It is a
-- one-time conversion of historical rows, and a fresh project has no
-- history to convert. Run it by hand later, only if you restore old data
-- that predates the Transfer type.
--
-- 0009 and 0010 belong together: 0009's revokes were ineffective on their
-- own (PostgreSQL's default EXECUTE grant to PUBLIC survived them) and
-- 0010 is what actually closes the hole. Do not run one without the other.
--
-- Every statement in here is idempotent (create ... if not exists, and
-- drop ... if exists before each policy and trigger), so re-running this
-- whole file is safe.
--
-- HOW TO RUN
--   Supabase dashboard -> SQL Editor -> New query -> paste -> Run.
--   Expect "Success. No rows returned."
--
-- AFTER RUNNING, these are NOT covered by this file and still need doing:
--   1. supabase/functions/coach  -> deploy it, and set OPENAI_API_KEY
--   2. Auth settings (email confirmations, redirect URLs) -> set in dashboard
--   3. personal_finance_app/.env -> point SUPABASE_URL / SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
--      at the new project
--
-- EXPECTED LEFTOVER WARNINGS from the security advisor afterwards: three
-- `authenticated can execute SECURITY DEFINER function` notices, for
-- seed_default_categories, rename_category and category_usage_count. Those
-- are by design — the app calls all three as a signed-in user, and each
-- filters on auth.uid() internally.
